draftpaper (0.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A login screen of Draftpaper's own, for greetd (#37, experimental):
    draftpaper-greeter draws the user's sheet, blurred, with a sign-in card
    on whole zones. With the name known a fingerprint reader (pam_fprintd)
    listens from the start; a password typed meanwhile is given when PAM
    asks. Signed in, the name slides into the title block's DRAWN cell and
    draftpaper-session starts on the same VT. `draftpaper apply` copies the
    sheet's look to /var/lib/draftpaper/greeter/<user>/ when that exists.
  * No flicker between the login screen and the session, either way. The
    compositor takes the displays over as they are (the same CRTC, the mode
    kept) instead of resetting them; leaving, it keeps its last frame on
    screen (CLOSEFB, Linux 6.8), a holder outside the login session keeping
    the device open until the next one draws. The next compositor opens on
    that very picture, read back from the display (and given as Smithay's
    first frame on the output), and reveals the desktop under it once the
    shell has lettered everything (IPC "ready"), the blur fading to nothing.
  * A frame the display refuses with a client's buffer on a plane (Firefox's
    hardware-decoded video on Intel) switches the outputs to composing
    everything, instead of a black or frozen screen.
  * Sound after logging in again: `draftpaper audio restore` restarts
    WirePlumber when only the dummy output is left (another session of the
    user's kept it running through the login screen, which took the card).
  * A session no longer opens a terminal at the start. The compositors log
    to the journal (journalctl -t draftpaper, -t draftpaper-greeter).
    `draftpaper-compositor grab DEVICE DIR [WIDTH]` (as root) reads back
    what each display shows.
  * The IPC's outputs list the layer surfaces on each; requests "blur",
    "ready" and, on the login screen, "leave".

 -- 0x64616e6e <daniel@este.systems>  Sat, 17 Oct 2026 05:13:04 +0300

draftpaper (0.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A launcher of its own on Mod+d (#34): the applications as a register in
    the frame's top-left corner, whole zones, what you type lettered in the
    margin above; fuzzy, the most used and recent first, launched through
    the compositor. `draftpaper menu` shows any list of lines in it for
    scripts. fuzzel moves to Suggests and stays an alternative.
  * The title block takes whole zones (the nearest to 130 by 36 mm: 3 by 1
    on the 4K and the laptop), its outer rule starting on the margins'
    ticks; its cells in proportion.
  * Free-form tiling: every window takes a rectangle of zones of its own.
    It moves a zone at a time keeping its size, into free zones, or past a
    window of the same rows (or columns), swapping places; anything else
    stops it. Mod+Ctrl+Right/Down make it a zone larger (into free zones,
    or from the windows there), Left/Up a zone smaller. A new window takes
    the largest free rectangle, else the second half of the focused one;
    a closed one leaves its zones to the windows that line up with it.
    `[layout] new_window` is gone.
  * The top and bottom margins number their zones (1 from the left), as the
    side margins letter theirs, in every zone nothing else takes.
  * Every window is a zone at least. A window alone on the sheet (or alone
    in its column) resizes in zones from the frame, Mod+Ctrl+Left/Up
    pulling its right or bottom edge in, Right/Down pushing it back out,
    and moves about the empty zones a zone at a time (Mod+Shift+arrows),
    keeping its size, before it moves to the next screen. A move with
    nowhere to go, or into a full column, changes nothing. With every zone
    taken, a new window floats.
  * Mod+z (the zoom action): a magnifier on the pointer, each pixel a
    block with a faint grid between them and the one under the pointer
    outlined, to check a line down to the pixel. Scrolling zooms (2 to 64
    times), Shift and scrolling sizes the lens (20 to 200 mm); Escape or
    Mod+z again puts it away.
  * draftpaper-compositor takes the displays and input devices only with
    --tty (as the session starts it): started from inside a session with
    no display to nest in, it no longer falls back to them.
  * draftpaper-compositor --headless WxH [--scale S]: one output drawn
    offscreen on the GPU's render node, shown nowhere, for tests; make
    pixels runs on it and needs no session. The pointer action moves the
    pointer (draftpaper-compositor msg pointer X Y).
  * The sheet is measured once, in the display's own pixels (title.rs): the
    frame, the margins' ticks, the title block's rules and cells, the
    wallpaper's frost. The compositor draws the title block's rules and the
    shell only letters its cells, so at a scale of 1.5 the block no longer
    runs a pixel past the frame into the margin, nor marks the frame's line
    where its top rule meets it; the frost stops at the frame's line, which
    keeps one colour along the block; the ticks at the frame's corners lie
    on the frame's line, not a pixel outside it. At a scale that does not
    divide the display (1.25, 1.75) the frame is measured from the display's
    edge, and the wallpaper covers its pixels exactly. make pixels checks it
    all, pixel by pixel, at every scale.
  * Tiles are measured with the sheet too, in the display's pixels, and each
    window is put on its own: its border a logical pixel's worth of whole
    pixels, against the frame's line, and between two windows the borders
    either side of the tick's pixel, which shows the paper in line with the
    tick in the margin. At 1.25 and 1.75 the windows on the right and at
    the bottom no longer run past the frame by a pixel or two, and at 1.5
    their edges and borders no longer fall between pixels. make pixels
    checks two windows at every scale.
  * The draftpaper command is Rust (cli/), no longer Python: the same
    commands and the same files, written byte for byte as before, and
    fast (the shell's readings every 5 s: 4 ms instead of 116; a click in
    the settings: 9 ms instead of 92). The package no longer needs python3.
  * A click on a layer surface (the settings, the launcher, the shell's
    margins, a panel's menu) no longer hands the keyboard to the window
    under it: a surface that reads keys takes it, else it stays where it
    was. The settings lost every second click to it (Qt cancelled the
    press as the keyboard left); they now also act on the press.
  * The title block draws its outer rule on its top and left only: its
    right and bottom are the frame's line, which its rule (in the theme's
    ink) covered when the margins take a wallpaper's colours.
  * The wallpaper on a scaled display (1.5, 2) showed its top-left corner,
    magnified: drawn without a source rectangle, Smithay took the logical
    size for one. Now the whole picture is drawn, and the ambient margins
    and the title block too. The title block's frost is lighter (about
    1.5 mm of blur), so the picture's shapes show through it.
  * The wallpaper's mounting and look: wallpaper_fit (fill, as before; fit
    whole on the paper; centre at its own size), wallpaper_position,
    wallpaper_brightness, wallpaper_wash (paper over it) and wallpaper_blur
    (mm), applied once when the picture is prepared, the ambient margins
    and the title block taking their colours from the result; and
    wallpaper_dirs, where the settings look for pictures. All on the
    wallpaper page too.
  * Settings: a wallpaper page (Tab): the pictures as drawings
    (~/Pictures/Wallpapers, the system's backgrounds, none), the margin
    cells in paper or the picture's colours, the title block's tint; each
    change shown at once, Escape putting back what there was. Setting a key
    keeps the comment after it, and takes the place of its commented-out
    example in config.toml.
  * Settings: one terminal row of mid tones (charcoal, graphite, slate,
    petrol, moss, olive, umber, wine, plum, or as the windows), in place
    of near-black tints and a separate terminal shade; the settings take a
    click at once and redraw only what changed.
  * wlr-output-management: kanshi, wlr-randr and wdisplays change the
    displays' modes, positions, scales and transforms, and turn them off and
    on; displays are named with the make, model and serial from their EDID
    (#30).
  * A screen's zones follow a change of its scale at once.
  * [layout] new_window: a third window opens a new column (as before) or a
    row under the focused window (#12).
  * Touchscreens: a finger goes to the surface under it (wl_touch), and
    a tap focuses the window as a click does; the touch follows the
    laptop panel's rotation.
  * A window of one fixed size (swappy's) floats, centred at its size,
    rather than leaving empty paper in a tile.
  * Window rules ([[rule]] in config.toml): match a window's app id or
    title (patterns) or a browser's picture-in-picture, and float it, keep
    it on every workspace (sticky), place it centred or in the corner, or
    open it without the focus. Built in: picture-in-picture (Meet's, a
    video's) floats in the bottom-right corner, sticky, leaving the focus.
  * A window whose maximum size is below its tile floats rather than
    leaving paper around it.
  * A floating window's border shows over the windows under it (it was
    drawn behind every window, so only tiles, bordered in the gutters,
    showed theirs).
  * Settings on the board (Mod+t, draftpaper settings; it replaces the
    theme picker): the inside of the frame cleared to the sheet's paper
    (with a wallpaper, the picture blurred), the themes as drawings with
    their contrasts, and a colour scheme built from nine choices whose
    lightnesses are solved so that every word reads at 4.5:1 or more
    (#36); a built scheme is kept in [scheme] and the theme "custom".
  * draftpaper theme check: themes against the contrast rules, run by
    make check on the shipped ones. Soft Glow's sheet ink darkened to
    6.6:1, Day Panel's terminal red lightened to 4.5:1.
  * Mod+Ctrl+h/j/k/l resize, as the arrows do.
  * A cheatsheet: every key, click, command and file on one page
    (/usr/share/doc/draftpaper/cheatsheet.md).
  * draftpaper-as USER [COMMAND]: a program, or a shell, as another user on
    the session, for work kept under a separate account.
  * draftpaper-as starts that user's service manager when it is not
    running: started from the desktop, sudo makes no login, and a program
    with no runtime directory has no D-Bus, portal or keyring.

 -- 0x64616e6e <daniel@este.systems>  Thu, 15 Oct 2026 03:15:13 +0300

draftpaper (0.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Draftpaper is now a Wayland compositor of its own (Smithay), started as
    the "Draftpaper" session from the display manager; the i3 edition is
    dropped (#31).
  * The sheet's margins, title block, side cells and notifications are drawn
    by the shell over the compositor's IPC; merged cells, Nerd Font glyphs.
  * Parking in the side margins, animated; focus follows the mouse.
  * A wallpaper in the frame, with the paper's or the picture's colours in the
    margins.
  * Screenshots, wlr-screencopy (grim, slurp) and screen sharing through
    xdg-desktop-portal-wlr; data control (clipboard managers); fractional
    scaling; linux-dmabuf; Xwayland.
  * Locking (ext-session-lock: swaylock), idle (swayidle): the screens off
    after 15 minutes, locking before sleep on request.
  * Volume and brightness keys; the volume is kept from one session to the
    next.
  * A theme picker (Super+T) with a live preview; a theme also colours
    alacritty, foot, tmux, Neovim, Emacs, GTK 3 and 4, Qt and Firefox (#35).

